Yesterday the Investment Committee approved IC-2026-008 by a 7-0 vote. The En=
ergy Infrastructure Fund moves from 5% to 7% in the Growth models and in the =
Balanced models, funded from fixed income as in February (Municipal Income Fu=
nd in taxable accounts, Core Fixed Income Fund elsewhere). Trading runs June =
1-12 and covers about 31 accounts and roughly $6.5 million of purchases.

Talking points:
- The thesis is data center power demand. Power demand from AI data centers i=
s growing faster than the grid can add capacity, and we expect much of the ne=
w supply to come from gas-fired generation.
- New gas plants need gas. We expect pipeline operators such as Williams Comp=
anies and Kinder Morgan to move higher volumes through their natural gas pipe=
lines under largely fee-based contracts - a volume story, not a commodity-pri=
ce bet.
- Power producers already in the fund, such as Constellation Energy and Vistr=
a, should also benefit from tighter power markets.
- Risks to acknowledge: rate sensitivity, project delays and policy changes.

Background reading: Natalie Brooks's January report "Powering AI: Data Center=
 Electricity Demand and the Grid" and Wade Pruitt's April report "Natural Gas=
 Midstream: The Data Center Power Trade".

